<h2>The idea beneath the Latin</h2>
<p>Adversarial litigation assumes two capable parties advancing their own interests. A four-year-old has interests but no voice; a person lacking mental capacity may have neither. Parens patriae fills that structural gap: the court steps into a protective role, gathers its own inputs — interviews in chambers, expert assessments, counsellors&rsquo; reports — and shapes relief around the protected person rather than around the winner of the pleadings. The doctrine is not a separate cause of action; it is a mode in which constitutional courts exercise the jurisdiction they already have.</p>

<h2>The doctrine at work in November 2026</h2>
<p>The Lersch judgment shows each element in operation. The dispute — between a German mother working in Singapore and an Indian father in Delhi over their son — arrived through a habeas corpus petition and an Article 136 appeal, while a guardianship petition pended separately. A conventional approach might have dismissed the writ as infructuous and remitted everyone to the guardianship court. Instead, the Court exercised parens patriae: it commissioned expert assessment of both parents and the child, interacted with the record itself, and settled a complete interim scheme — custody, a weekly schedule, foreign travel conditioned on a mirror order from a Singapore court, and a Rs. 1 crore deposit. Its articulation of the principle was unqualified: the child&rsquo;s best interests <em>&ldquo;must prevail over all other considerations,&rdquo;</em> and even international jurisdictional issues cannot stand in their way.</p>

<h2>Where practitioners meet the doctrine</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Habeas corpus for custody.</strong> The writ is maintainable between private parties where a child&rsquo;s detention is illegal; parens patriae converts it from a legality inquiry into a welfare inquiry.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Article 136 matters.</strong> The Supreme Court uses the doctrine to craft complete interim arrangements rather than remitting fragile situations to multi-year guardianship trials.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cross-border families.</strong> Welfare analysis, not the comity of foreign decrees alone, decides whether a child moves or stays — with mirror orders and deposits securing compliance abroad.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Incapacity cases.</strong> Beyond children, the doctrine grounds judicial decisions for persons unable to decide for themselves, from medical treatment to property protection.</div>
</div>

<h2>The discipline the doctrine demands</h2>
<p>Precisely because parens patriae loosens procedural constraints, courts bind themselves to evidence-rich, child-centred method: neutral expert evaluation, in-chambers interaction, counselling where a child&rsquo;s mind may have been turned, and interim rather than irreversible orders. For counsel, that sets the brief. Arguments framed as parental rights — possession, comity, forum — consistently lose to arguments framed as the child&rsquo;s stability, schooling, attachment and safety. The winning submission in a parens patriae case reads less like a pleading and more like a welfare plan the court can adopt, complete with the safeguards that make it enforceable.</p>
<div class="note">Parens patriae is a jurisdiction of last resort and careful hands: it does not replace the Guardians and Wards Act, the Family Courts Act or ordinary custody appeals, and interim arrangements made under it remain subject to the statutory court&rsquo;s final determination. Every invocation turns on the specific child&rsquo;s circumstances.</div>
